I think that when you pick up one of the 3 keys, you should get a message
like "You feel as if you should use this first. --more--" (or last, or
second, respectively); perhaps it would not be immediately obvious what the
message meant, but by the time you're ready to go find a vibrating square in
the lowest pits of Hell, you'll probably have figured it out. Perhaps each
item could say this each time you pick it up? That way, even if you forget,
you can drop the item and pick it up and figure out what's what.

Just a thought.

>it should be possible to get some information about artifacts,
>named weapons...as it is, there's no way to figure out that
>Excalibur protects from level drain (besides reading the
>spoilers or the code...) etc.

Again, I think that, upon wielding/picking up such items, they should give a
message about the various abilities that they confer. 

"A +0 elven dagger named Sting --more--"
"You feel alert. --more--"
"You feel a great hatred for orcs."
"You are now wielding a lamp. --more--"
"Your alertness fades. --more--"
"Orcs don't seem so bad anymore."

Perhaps the messages could be similar to, but not exactly the same as the
intrinsics they confer.

Also, perhaps one could get a warning (if non-Lawful) before picking up 
Excalibur, to the effect of: 

"Excalibur has killed those unworthies who have tried to handle it before.
--more--"
"Are you sure you want to pick this up? [yn] (n)"

If someone still goes for it, they deserve what they get.

